# What must never travel from a developer's machine into an image.
#
# Without this file, `COPY apps/client ./apps/client` brings the host's own build output
# and `node_modules/` along, and the build then ran on top of them. The result
# was an image whose server bundle and client assets came from different builds:
# the server-rendered HTML asked for /assets/styles-DLWCcDOO.css, that file was
# not there, and the 404 stopped the stylesheet blocking the paint — so every
# refresh showed the page with no CSS at all until the client JS loaded and
# fetched the right one about two seconds later.
#
# A stale asset is worse than a missing one. The build looked like it succeeded.

# Dependencies. Also built for the developer's OS — copying Windows or macOS
# binaries into a linux image is its own class of failure.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# Build output. Regenerated inside the image; carrying a copy in is what caused
# the mismatch above.
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/
.output/
.turbo/
**/.turbo/
.mastra/
**/.mastra/
**/.vinxi/
**/.nitro/
# Next's build output and its cache. The same lesson as `dist/` above, and far
# more expensive to learn: a developer's .next/ reached 1.2GB, and sending it as
# build context was enough to get the build killed outright before it compiled
# a single file.
.next/
**/.next/

# Python
.venv/
**/.venv/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
**/.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.model-cache/

# Secrets. None of the Dockerfiles copy a path that would reach the root .env
# today, but that is a property of the current COPY lines rather than a rule —
# one added line would change it, and an image layer keeps what it was given.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and local tooling
.git/
.gitignore
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/

# Not needed to build or run
*.md
!README.md
coverage/
**/coverage/
*.log
